# .gitleaksignore — per-finding allowlist for the secret-scan CI gate (gitleaks).
#
# Each line is a gitleaks FINGERPRINT (commit:path:rule:line). Listing a fingerprint
# silences ONLY that exact finding — every new or moved secret still trips the gate.
# This is the right tool for vetted false positives in HISTORY (test fixtures, doc
# placeholders, protocol examples) that predate the secret-scan gate and cannot be
# edited away without rewriting git history.
#
# RULE: only add a fingerprint here AFTER confirming by eye it is not a real secret.
# Never silence a real leak — rotate it and scrub it instead.
#
# PATH-VARIANT NOTE: the `path` segment of a fingerprint is how git resolves the file in
# that historical commit's diff — and that resolution DIFFERS between environments when the
# file was later renamed (this monorepo moved proxy/ -> server/proxy/, specs/ -> docs/specs/,
# components/ -> client/components/). macOS git (rename-detecting) reports the pre-rename
# path; the GitHub Actions runner reports the current path. An unmatched fingerprint is a
# harmless no-op, so we list BOTH variants for each finding to stay green in either env.
#
# All entries below are pre-existing findings in this repo's history, each verified by eye
# to be a non-secret (see annotation). The first line of each pair is the CI-authoritative
# (runner) path; the second is the local (rename-resolved) path.
#
# SCAN-MODE NOTE (HYP-935 fix-strategy rig-sync): secret-scan.yml switched from `gitleaks
# git` (history/diff scan, fingerprint = `<commit>:<path>:<rule>:<line>`) to `gitleaks dir .`
# (working-tree scan, fingerprint = `<path>:<rule>:<line>` — no commit, since there is no
# diff/history involved). The commit-prefixed entries below are now dead weight for the
# active `dir` scan (they still work if the workflow ever reverts to `gitleaks git`, so they
# are kept rather than deleted). Each finding below ALSO gets a commit-less `dir`-mode
# entry, using the file's CURRENT line number. Unlike the commit-pinned entries above, a
# `dir`-mode entry is NOT immune to future line-shifts in the same file — an edit above the
# line silently invalidates it (re-verify by eye and update the line if this ever recurs).

